(a) The human ovum just before fertilisation is drawn as a large round cell surrounded by a few sperms, with these labels (any four earn the marks):
- the **ovum** with its **nucleus**;
- the **vitelline membrane** (the plasma membrane, or egg membrane) around the ovum;
- the **perivitelline space** between the vitelline membrane and the zona pellucida;
- the **zona pellucida**, the thick non-cellular layer around it;
- the **cells of the corona radiata**, the follicle cells forming the outermost layer.

(b) During fertilisation, as soon as a sperm comes in contact with the **zona pellucida** layer of the ovum, it **induces changes in the membrane that block the entry of additional sperms**. So only one sperm can fertilise an ovum, and polyspermy is avoided.

(a) pBR322 is drawn as a circular plasmid with these four regions labelled:
- (i) **ori**, the origin of replication;
- (ii) \(\text{amp}^{\text{R}}\), the ampicillin resistance gene;
- (iii) \(\text{tet}^{\text{R}}\), the tetracycline resistance gene;
- (iv) **rop**, which codes for the proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid.

The restriction sites (such as BamH I and Sal I within \(\text{tet}^{\text{R}}\), Pst I and Pvu I within \(\text{amp}^{\text{R}}\), and EcoR I) may also be shown, though the question asks only for the four labels.

(b) The **origin of replication (ori)** is the sequence from where replication starts. Any piece of DNA, when linked to this sequence, can be made to replicate within the host cell. This sequence is also responsible for **controlling the copy number** of the linked DNA, so a vector with a suitable ori can be used to obtain many copies of the target DNA.
